theory RHTNCurrency
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN currency attestation + stapling -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHAT THIS FILE IS
  design Section 12.6.5 replaces revocation with short-lived patron-signed
  CURRENCY ATTESTATIONS, stapled to introductions.  The review plan's Stage
  1.1 target: "a revoked key cannot be made to appear current beyond the
  attestation lifetime".

  THAT TARGET IS NOT CLOSED HERE, and the introduction used to say it was.
  What is proved is narrower and worth stating exactly: a relying party
  accepts a key as current only if the patron signed an attestation for THAT
  EXACT key and epoch, and no expiry for that epoch was declared first.  Two
  things the target's wording implies are absent --

    * ROTATION.  There is no current-key state, so "once the patron rotates
      the subject to a new key" has no representation at all.  That is not an
      oversight: both halves of design Section 12.6.5's supersession rule are
      unenforceable client behaviour, which the note above
      currency_requires_unexpired_issuance sets out in full.
    * TIME.  "Beyond the attestation lifetime" has no meaning here.  Expiry
      is an event that may or may not occur, plus a restriction discarding
      acceptances after it; nothing makes an attestation expire, so a trace
      may accept one at an arbitrarily late position.  What is proved is
      "an epoch DECLARED expired is not subsequently accepted", never "a
      lifetime elapsed".

  The lemma is about which key a relying party takes as current -- the one
  thing a staple decides; nothing the party then does waits on it (design
  Section 12.6.5) -- and it does
  exercise the "issue fresh, never extend stale" rule (design Section
  12.6.5.1) in the sense that every issuance signs a new attestation rather
  than restamping an old one.

TIME, SYMBOLICALLY
  Tamarin has no clock.  We model expiry as EVENT ORDER using action facts
  and a restriction, the standard idiom (as in the plan's note that "expiry
  is event ordering, not clocks"):
    * an attestation is minted with an Issued action carrying an epoch term;
    * an Expire action can fire for an epoch;
    * a restriction forbids ACCEPTING an attestation whose epoch has expired
      before the acceptance.
  "Epoch" is an abstract token, not a number -- we need only the ordering
  "issued, then (maybe) expired, then someone tries to rely on it".

  Reading conventions (rule shape, fact sorts, ~/$ prefixes, the trace,
  temporal logic) are spelled out in attach.spthy; only NEW idioms are
  re-explained here.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: signing, hashing

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IDENTITIES AND KEYS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A patron and the subjects it vouches for.  The patron has a long-term
  signing key; a subject is represented by a KEY it currently holds.  A
  rotation gives the subject a new key and (per the design) the patron will
  issue attestations for the new key, not the old.
*/

rule Register_Patron:
    [ Fr(~psk), Fr(~ppq) ]
  --[ PatronReg($P), OnlyOnce($P) ]->
    [ !PatronKey($P, ~psk)             // the patron's long-term secret key
    , !Kh($P, h(<pk(~psk), pk(~ppq)>))  // the identity IS the keyhash over the
                                        //   pair, even though an attestation
                                        //   is signed classically only
    , Out(<pk(~psk), pk(~ppq)>) ]

// A subject's key.  The `key` term ~sk stands for the whole key material
// the keyhash commits to; $S is the subject identity.  A subject may hold
// several keys over time (rotation); each is a distinct fresh value.
rule Subject_Key:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ SubjectKey($S, ~sk) ]->
    [ !SubjKey($S, ~sk)                // a key $S holds (persistent)
    , Out(pk(~sk)) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EPOCHS -- the abstract clock
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A fresh epoch token is drawn to stamp each attestation.  An epoch may
  later Expire.  The ORDER of Issued / Expire / Accept actions on the trace
  is the entire content of "lifetime": we never compare durations, only
  whether an expiry preceded an acceptance.
*/

/*  TWO SIGNED TIMESTAMPS, NOT ONE TOKEN.  `wire-format.md`'s attestation
    carries field 3 `issued_at` and field 4 `expires_at` as SEPARATE signed
    fields, and the signature covers both.  Collapsing them into a single
    epoch made the expiry boundary unrepresentable as a signed value: a
    validator that reconstructed the payload without field 4, letting an
    intermediary move `expires_at`, had no trace.  The ordering abstraction
    stays coarse -- there is still no arithmetic on time -- but the two
    fields are now distinct terms inside the signature, so omitting or
    substituting either is mutation-testable.                              */
rule New_Epoch:
    [ Fr(~iat), Fr(~exp) ]
  --[ EpochStart(~iat, ~exp) ]->
    [ !Epoch(~iat, ~exp), Out(<~iat, ~exp>) ]   // both are public

rule Expire_Epoch:
    [ !Epoch(~iat, ~exp) ]
  --[ Expired(~exp) ]->                // trace: this expiry boundary is past
    [ ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ISSUING AN ATTESTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The patron signs <'currency', subject, subjectKey, epoch>.  Binding the
  SUBJECT KEY into the signature is the crux: an attestation vouches for a
  specific key (design Section 12.6.5's `current_key` field), so it cannot
  be transplanted onto a different key.  Binding the EPOCH lets expiry bite.

  "Issue fresh, never extend stale" (design Section 12.6.5.1) is modelled by
  every issuance signing afresh: the patron cannot re-stamp an
  old attestation with a new epoch without signing a new one, and signing a
  new one is exactly issuing fresh.  NOTE WHAT "CURRENT" DOES NOT MEAN HERE:
  epochs are persistent and several unexpired ones can coexist, so an issuance
  draws SOME unexpired epoch rather than a uniquely current one.  What
  IssueFreshNotStale forbids is stamping an epoch already expired.
*/

/*
  THE ATTESTED FIELD IS PUBLIC, AND MODELLING IT AS A SECRET BROKE THE WHOLE
  THEORY.  `wire-format.md` §7.1 and design §12.6.5: a CurrencyAttestation
  carries `current_key: keyhash` -- a public commitment to key material, not
  the material itself.  An earlier version signed over the fresh secret ~sk
  and emitted only the signature, so a relying party could never assemble the
  tuple Accept_Currency demands: nothing ever output ~sk.

  THE CONSEQUENCE WAS NOT A FAILING LEMMA BUT A HOLLOW ONE.  currency_is_usable
  still "verified" -- via Compromise_Patron, the adversary forging an
  attestation with a key it stole.  So the anti-vacuity guard was satisfied by
  a compromised trace, and currency_requires_unexpired_issuance was satisfied
  by its own compromise disjunct.  Three green lemmas, none of them about the
  honest protocol.  currency_is_usable_honestly below is the guard that
  regression cannot pass: an acceptance with NO compromise anywhere.

  Issuance now emits the whole attestation object, as a staple is (design
  §12.6.5: the introducing node "staples a recent patron-signed currency
  attestation to its introduction").
*/
/*  A SCOPE LIMIT ON THE ISSUER, recorded because a green lemma here reads
    stronger than it is.  Any `!PatronKey($P, ~psk)` may issue for any
    `!SubjKey($S, sk)`: there is no `PatronOf($P, $S)` relation and no issuer
    ROLE, whereas the wire object carries both an issuer identity and its
    role -- patron, sibling, grandpatron or down-line (design 12.6.5.1).

    So what the lemmas below establish is "the P whose key signed this tuple
    issued it", NOT "P was an authorised issuer for S by any of the paths the
    ladder allows".  A relying party inside the horizon can check the latter
    from its own topology; one outside cannot, which is part of why the
    design puts the escalation ladder's correctness in
    `tla/CurrencyEscalation.tla` -- where the rungs and their conditions ARE
    modelled -- rather than here.

    Closing it in this theory means adding a topology relation and stating
    the lemmas conditional on it.  Not done; recorded.                     */
/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHO MAY ISSUE -- `wire-format.md`'s CurrencyAttestation fields 5 and 6
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The attestation names its ISSUER (field 6) and the ROLE that issuer claims
  (field 5: 0 patron, 1 sibling/secondhand, 2 grandpatron, 3 down-line
  threshold).  Both sit inside the signature.

  NOTHING STOPS A KEYHOLDER ISSUING.  The rule below takes the role from the
  network, because field 5 is a CLAIM: any party holding a key can sign an
  attestation calling itself anyone's patron.  The wire cannot prevent that
  and this theory does not pretend otherwise.

  THE CHECK IS THE RELYING PARTY'S, AGAINST ITS OWN RECORDS.  `!Authorised`
  is indexed by the RELYING party as well as the issuer, because trust is
  per-observer (design Section 16.1) and no topology is shared.  A party
  inside the horizon holds the adoption records that say who may issue for
  whom; one outside does not, and has no basis to accept on this ground.
*/

rule Records_Patron:
    [ ] --[ AuthorisedBy($R, $I, $S, 'patron') ]->
        [ !Authorised($R, $I, $S, 'patron') ]

rule Records_Sibling:
    [ ] --[ AuthorisedBy($R, $I, $S, 'sibling') ]->
        [ !Authorised($R, $I, $S, 'sibling') ]

rule Records_Grandpatron:
    [ ] --[ AuthorisedBy($R, $I, $S, 'grandpatron') ]->
        [ !Authorised($R, $I, $S, 'grandpatron') ]

rule Records_Downline:
    [ ] --[ AuthorisedBy($R, $I, $S, 'downline') ]->
        [ !Authorised($R, $I, $S, 'downline') ]

rule Issue_Currency:
    [ !PatronKey($I, ~isk)             // ANY keyholder -- see above
    , !SubjKey($S, sk)                 // for a key the subject holds
    , !Epoch(iat, exp)                 // stamped with both timestamps
    , In(role) ]                       // claiming whatever role it likes
  --[ Issued($I, $S, pk(sk), iat, exp)
    , IssuedAs($I, $S, role)
    , IssuedAttestation($I, $S, pk(sk), iat, exp, role)
    , IssuedFresh($I, $S, exp) ]->
    [ Out(<'currency', $S, pk(sk), iat, exp, role, $I,
           sign(<'rhtn/1:currency', <$S, pk(sk), iat, exp, role, $I>>, ~isk)>) ]


/*  ISSUING WITHOUT THE MODELLED STEP.  `Issue_Currency` above requires a
    `!SubjKey`, so every attestation in the model named a key the subject
    really held.  Nothing on the wire enforces that: an issuer can attest any
    keyhash it likes for any subject.  This rule says so, and the lemmas
    below are unaffected because they speak about what was SIGNED, not about
    which transition produced it.                                          */
rule Issue_Currency_Arbitrary:
    [ !PatronKey($I, ~isk)
    , !Epoch(iat, exp)
    , In(<$S, kh, role>) ]
  --[ Issued($I, $S, kh, iat, exp)
    , IssuedAs($I, $S, role)
    , IssuedAttestation($I, $S, kh, iat, exp, role)
    , SignedWithoutTransition($I) ]->
    [ Out(<'currency', $S, kh, iat, exp, role, $I,
           sign(<'rhtn/1:currency', <$S, kh, iat, exp, role, $I>>, ~isk)>) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  RELYING ON AN ATTESTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A relying party takes subject $S as current under key sk -- the key it
  will address -- having checked a purported attestation from patron $P.
  Two things gate that acceptance:
    * the signature verifies (the Eq restriction, as in attach.spthy);
    * the epoch has NOT expired before this acceptance (the NotExpired
      restriction below).
  Nothing the party then does waits on it: a staple decides which key to
  address and gates no operation (design Section 12.6.5).
*/

/*  ACCEPTANCE READS THE TIMESTAMPS FROM THE ATTESTATION, not from a registry.
    It used to take `!Epoch(iat, exp)` as a premise, which re-bound the two to
    a pair the environment had minted together -- so an attestation signed for
    one expiry could not be presented with another, and the binding of field 4
    was enforced by the MODEL rather than by the signature.  A validator does
    not consult such a registry; it reads both fields off the object and
    relies on the signature covering them.  With the premise gone, omitting
    `expires_at` from the signed payload is mutation-testable.            */
rule Accept_Currency:
    // The staple arrives carrying its fields AND the signature over them
    // (design Section 12.6.5: the introducing node "staples a recent
    // patron-signed currency attestation to its introduction").  Taking the
    // fields from the message is also what BINDS them: an earlier version
    // read `sk` from nowhere, which Tamarin reports as an unbound variable
    // and unintended pattern matching.
    [ In(<'currency', $S, sk, iat, exp, role, $I, att>)  // the staple, as received
    , !Kh($I, khI)                     // the ISSUER's identity, and
    , In(kmI)                          //   CANDIDATE KeyMaterial for it
    , !Authorised($R, $I, $S, role) ] // ... and $R's OWN record that this
                                       //   issuer may act in this role for $S
  --[ Eq(h(kmI), khI)                // BOUND BEFORE USE.  Looking the issuer's
                                       //   key up by identity made the
                                       //   identity-to-key relation true
                                       //   before validation; a validator
                                       //   omitting the check had no trace.
    , Eq(verify(att, <'rhtn/1:currency', <$S, sk, iat, exp, role, $I>>, fst(kmI)), true)
    , NotExpiredAt(exp)                  // guarded by the restriction below
    , Accepted($R, $I, $S, sk, iat, exp, role) ]->
    [ ]

/*
  ONE REGISTRATION PER NAME.  A public label like $A stands for a keyhash,
  and design Section 5.1 makes a keyhash a commitment to the key material --
  change the material and you have a different identity.  Without the
  restriction below, Register could fire twice for one label and give a
  single symbolic name two unrelated keys, so "the party I meant to reach"
  would not pin a key.  (currency.spthy's Subject_Key is deliberately NOT
  restricted this way: a subject holding several keys over time is rotation,
  which is that theory's subject matter.)
*/
restriction OneKeyPerName:
  "All a #i #j. OnlyOnce(a) @ #i & OnlyOnce(a) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

// The expiry rule: an acceptance stamped NotExpiredAt(e) is only
// allowed on traces where no Expired(e) happened BEFORE it.  This is how
// "current vs expired" becomes event order without a clock.
//
// THIS IS AN AXIOM, NOT A RESULT, and the difference matters when reading
// the lemma below.  A restriction DISCARDS traces; it does not show the
// protocol prevents them.  What is modelled here is a relying party with a
// trustworthy clock that refuses an expired staple -- an environment
// assumption, exactly like the co-presence axiom in ceremony.spthy.  So
// currency_requires_unexpired_issuance holds *relative to* clock
// enforcement; it is not evidence that clock enforcement happens.  Nor does
// expiry_is_reachable establish that every issued attestation eventually
// expires -- only that an expiry can occur, so the restriction is not
// vacuously satisfied by nothing ever expiring.
// "ISSUE FRESH, NEVER EXTEND STALE" (design §12.6.5.1), modelled rather than
// asserted.  !Epoch is persistent, so without this a patron could keep
// stamping an epoch that had already expired -- which is exactly what
// extending stale state looks like, and the file's own comment claimed every
// issuance draws a CURRENT epoch when nothing enforced it.  The acceptance
// restriction below does not cover this: it blocks accepting an expired
// attestation, not minting one.
// WHAT PERSISTENCE ALSO PERMITS, and why that is not the rule's concern: the
// same current (iat, exp) pair stamped twice.  That is two attestations over
// one window, equivalent to one, and the wire's rule is about EXTENSION --
// "there is no 'extend' operation and no field for one" (`wire-format.md`
// Section 7) -- not about issue times being distinct.  A cross-family review
// raised the stricter reading and did not count it; recorded here so the next
// one need not.
/*  "ISSUE FRESH; NEVER EXTEND STALE" IS A CONFORMING ISSUER'S BEHAVIOUR, and
    this restriction now says only that.  It used to quantify over `Issued`,
    which BOTH issuance rules emit, so it also made a dishonest keyholder
    cryptographically unable to sign a stale epoch -- an inability no wire
    format confers.  A conforming issuer refuses; anyone else may sign what
    they like and the acceptance side is what rejects it.                   */
restriction IssueFreshNotStale:
  "All P S e #j. IssuedFresh(P, S, e) @ #j
     ==> not(Ex #x. Expired(e) @ #x & #x < #j)"

restriction NotExpiredBeforeAccept:
  "All e #a. NotExpiredAt(e) @ #a
     ==> not(Ex #x. Expired(e) @ #x & #x < #a)"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// SANITY: the honest flow runs -- a patron issues, a party accepts.
lemma currency_is_usable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex R I S sk iat exp role #i. Accepted(R, I, S, sk, iat, exp, role) @ #i"

// THE GUARD THAT ACTUALLY GUARDS.  An acceptance with NO compromise anywhere
// on the trace, resting on a genuine issuance.  currency_is_usable above does
// NOT establish this and never did: it was satisfied by a trace in which the
// patron's key was stolen and the adversary forged the attestation, so every
// lemma below it was about compromised traces.  A vacuity guard that a
// compromised trace can satisfy is not a vacuity guard.
lemma currency_is_usable_honestly:
  exists-trace
  "Ex R I S kh iat exp role #j #i.
       Issued(I, S, kh, iat, exp) @ #j
     & Accepted(R, I, S, kh, iat, exp, role) @ #i
     & #j < #i
     & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k)
     & not(Ex X #t. SignedWithoutTransition(X) @ #t)"

// SANITY: an epoch can actually expire, so the NotExpiredBeforeAccept
// restriction is not vacuously satisfiable by nothing ever expiring.
// NOTE WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY: it asserts only that Expired(e) occurs.  It
// does not exhibit an acceptance alongside it, and an earlier comment here
// claimed it did.
lemma expiry_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex e #x. Expired(e) @ #x"

// THE SECURITY PROPERTY (Stage 1.1).  Every acceptance of a key sk as
// current rests on a genuine, unexpired patron issuance for that
// exact key -- OR the patron's key was compromised.  Because Accept is
// gated by NotExpiredBeforeAccept, an EXPIRED attestation cannot be
// accepted; because the signature binds sk, an attestation for one key
// cannot be replayed onto another.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE, AND WHY THAT IS CORRECT RATHER THAN MISSING.
// The lemma establishes that an accepted (S, sk, e) was signed for that same
// (S, sk, e) and had not expired.  It does NOT establish that sk is the
// subject's CURRENT key, and no protocol model can, because currency is not
// a protocol property.
//
// design Section 12.6.5 keeps expiry and supersession apart -- "Expiry
// bounds what a stolen credential can spend; supersession, once known, is
// what retires it from use" -- and BOTH HALVES OF THE SUPERSESSION RULE ARE
// CLIENT BEHAVIOUR [author, 2026-09-05]:
//
//   * the ISSUER'S half, "issue only for the key you currently record", is
//     unenforceable because the attestation names a subject, a key and an
//     epoch and carries nothing distinguishing one issued before a rotation
//     from one issued after;
//   * the RELYING PARTY'S half, "stop serving a binding you have verified
//     superseded", is unenforceable for the ordinary reason that it is a
//     rule about what a party does with its own records.
//
// AND THE TWO NEVER MEET.  A party that could detect a stale issuance is one
// that holds the recovery adoption -- and such a party has already
// overwritten its own record (design Section 9.0.2), so it rejects on that
// and never consults the staple.  A party that would consult the staple is
// outside the horizon, where by design old and new keys are separate
// entities and there is nothing to detect.
//
// So there is no third-party-checkable property here for Tamarin to have.
// What the protocol enforces is the signature and the epoch, and that is
// what is proved.  The obligations live where obligations live:
// `infra-client-requirements.md` Section 3 for the issuer,
// Section 2 for the relying party.  design Section 1.1's test --
// a rule aimed at a party you share no state with is a wish -- is the whole
// of the answer, and a rebuild attempted on 2026-09-05 was machinery for a
// property that was never the protocol's to have.
lemma currency_requires_unexpired_issuance:
  "All R I S sk iat exp role #i.
     Accepted(R, I, S, sk, iat, exp, role) @ #i
     ==> ( ( (Ex #j. Issued(I, S, sk, iat, exp) @ #j & #j < #i)
           & not(Ex #x. Expired(exp) @ #x & #x < #i) )
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(I) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

/*  ISSUED_AT IS GUARDED TOO, and until 2026-09-07 it was not.  Both
    timestamps sat inside the signed term and the note above claimed
    "omitting or substituting either is mutation-testable" -- but only `exp`
    appeared in `Issued` and `Accepted`, so no property had an `iat` value to
    compare.  An implementation family that consistently left `issued_at` out
    of the signed payload, at issuer and validator alike, contradicted
    nothing.  Now the accepted value must be the issued one.              */
lemma the_accepted_issue_time_is_the_signed_one:
  "All R I S sk iat exp role #i.
     Accepted(R, I, S, sk, iat, exp, role) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #j. Issued(I, S, sk, iat, exp) @ #j & #j < #i)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(I) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

/*  THE ISSUER AND ROLE ARE INSIDE THE SIGNATURE.  An attestation issued as a
    sibling cannot be re-presented as a patron's, and one issuer's cannot be
    attributed to another.  `wire-format.md` puts fields 5 and 6 under the
    signature for exactly this; drop either from the signed tuple and this
    lemma falsifies.                                                        */
/*  ONE ISSUANCE EVENT, NOT TWO CORRELATED BY COINCIDENCE.  This lemma used
    `IssuedAs(I, S, role)`, which carries no key and no timestamps -- so a
    DIFFERENT attestation from the same issuer, for the same subject, in that
    role, discharged it.  Together with `currency_requires_unexpired_issuance`
    witnessed by the accepted key's own `Issued(...)`, the pair could be
    satisfied by two separate attestations, and neither lemma noticed that the
    accepted role had never been signed over the accepted key.

    `IssuedAttestation` carries the whole signed tuple -- fields 1 to 6 -- so
    the property now says the ACCEPTED attestation was issued, not that
    something like it was.                                                 */
lemma an_attestation_binds_its_issuer_and_role:
  "All R I S sk iat exp role #i.
     Accepted(R, I, S, sk, iat, exp, role) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #j. IssuedAttestation(I, S, sk, iat, exp, role) @ #j & #j < #i)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(I) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

/*  AND THE RELYING PARTY CHECKED ITS OWN RECORDS.  Acceptance names an issuer
    that THIS party records as authorised for THIS subject in THIS role.  Not
    a claim that the issuer really was authorised -- no such global fact
    exists -- but that the accepting party consulted its own topology rather
    than the attestation's say-so.  Delete the `!Authorised` premise from
    Accept_Currency and this falsifies.                                     */
/*  "RECORDED", NOT "CURRENTLY AUTHORISES", and the difference is real.
    `!Authorised` is persistent here and nothing withdraws it, so what this
    establishes is that the accepting party recorded this issuer in this role
    at some earlier point -- not that the relationship still holds when the
    attestation is accepted.  The roles name CURRENT relationships (design
    12.6.5.1's ladder moves among them as topology changes), so the gap is
    real rather than notational.

    IT IS CLOSED IN TLA+, not here.  Making the record revocable means a
    capability that is read many times and withdrawn once, and Tamarin's
    backward search does not terminate on that shape -- the same
    consume-and-restore regress diagnosed for currency issuance and session
    serving.  `tla/IssuerAuthorisation.tla` checks the lifecycle exhaustively.  */
lemma acceptance_names_an_issuer_this_party_recorded_as_authorised:
  "All R I S sk iat exp role #i.
     Accepted(R, I, S, sk, iat, exp, role) @ #i
     ==> Ex #a. AuthorisedBy(R, I, S, role) @ #a & #a < #i"

// PATRON COMPROMISE, made available so the carve-out above is real rather
// than decorative: the adversary can steal the patron's key, exactly as in
// attach.spthy.  Without this rule the second disjunct could never be
// witnessed and we would not know the first disjunct is doing the work.
rule Compromise_Patron:
    [ !PatronKey($P, ~psk) ]
  --[ Compromised($P) ]->
    [ Out(~psk) ]

end
